---
import { css } from 'styled-system/css'
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import TableScroll from '../../../components/TableScroll.astro'
import { REPO_URL } from '../../../data/docs'

const toc = [
  { label: 'What the license gives you', href: '#what-you-get' },
  { label: 'Obligations', href: '#obligations' },
  { label: 'Where the license lives', href: '#where' },
  { label: 'Third-party code', href: '#third-party' },
  { label: 'Contributions', href: '#contributions' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="License"
  description="todo-lofi is free software under the GNU Affero General Public License v3.0. Here is what that means in practice."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="warning" title="The license text is authoritative">
    This page summarizes the AGPL-3.0; it is not a substitute for it and it is not legal advice. The full
    text is in <code>LICENSE</code> at the repository root.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="what-you-get">What the license gives you</h2>
  <p>
    You may run the software for any purpose, study and modify it, and redistribute it — commercially or
    not — provided you pass on the same freedoms to everyone downstream.
  </p>

  <h2 id="obligations">Obligations</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Share the source.</strong> If you distribute the software, or a modified version of it, you
      must make the complete corresponding source available under the AGPL-3.0.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Network use counts.</strong> Running a modified version as a service over a network requires
      offering the source to the people using that service. This is the clause that separates the AGPL
      from the GPL.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keep the notices.</strong> Copyright notices and the license text stay in place, and
      significant changes must be stated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No warranty.</strong> The software is provided as is, with no warranty, as the license
      states.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you want to use the code under different terms — for example, in a closed product you cannot
    license as AGPL — you need a separate agreement from the copyright holders.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where">Where the license lives</h2>
  <TableScroll label="Where the license lives">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Path</th>
          <th>What it is</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><code>LICENSE</code></td>
          <td>The full AGPL-3.0 text, at the repository root.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><code>Cargo.toml</code></td>
          <td>The workspace manifest; each crate inherits the workspace license field.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    The canonical text is also published by the
    <a href="https://www.gnu.org/licenses/agpl-3.0.html">Free Software Foundation</a>, and the matching
    identifier is <code>AGPL-3.0</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="third-party">Third-party code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Dependencies pulled from crates.io or git keep their own licenses; the AGPL covers this
      repository's code, not theirs.
    </li>
    <li>
      The vendored forks under <code>patched/</code> (for example the cersei provider and agent forks)
      retain their upstream licenses. Read the license in each patched crate before reusing it.
    </li>
    <li>
      The self-hosted font in the web design system is Inter Variable, under the SIL Open Font License.
    </li>
    <li>
      The web design system's icons and copy are placeholders written for this repository. It reproduces no
      third-party wordmark, screenshot, or marketing copy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="contributions">Contributions</h2>
  <p>
    By contributing you agree that your contribution is licensed under the same terms, so the project can
    keep offering one consistent license to everyone. There is no separate CLA.
  </p>
  <p class={css({ fontSize: 'sm', color: 'fg.subtle' })}>
    See <a href={`${REPO_URL}/blob/main/LICENSE`}>LICENSE</a> for the complete terms, and
    <a href="/docs/contributor/contributing">Contributing</a> for how to propose a change.
  </p>
</Docs>
